---
// FAQ accordion — native details/summary, no JavaScript. Answers are the
// author's own words.
---

<div class="faq" data-faq>
  <details name="faq">
    <summary>What is a doctor?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      A doctor is a single JS file that scans your repo for mistakes and
      reports the findings. You can create your own doctors for specific
      patterns in your own codebase and re-use them later on or share with
      your team.
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>Why "doctor"?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      Heavily inspired by the react-doctor experience. "What if we had a
      'doctor' for everything we cared about?"
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>What's the health score?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      A 0–100 number at the end of a run. Severity is weighted across
      findings and files with a grade.
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>How is this different from ESLint?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      You should still 100% be using linters. any-doctor is designed for
      recurring patterns that show up in your code that you don't
      necessarily want to make lint configs for.
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>How is this different from AI code reviewers?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      You should still run your regular AI code reviewers. However, you can
      save your agents tokens and time by having them run doctors. Doctors
      should be calling out bad patterns from mistakes you've seen them make
      so they can focus on the real issues.
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>What about false positives?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      You or your agent can accept findings as intended so it never shows
      again.
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>Does it send my code anywhere?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>No.</p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>How do I write my own?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      Describe the patterns or bugs you want to catch with any-doctor to
      your agent. The doctor contract is one file with named checks and
      evidence rules. A full agent guide is baked into the CLI.
    </p></div>
  </details>

  <details name="faq">
    <summary>Can it run in CI?<svg class="chev" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></summary>
    <div class="a"><p>
      Yes — though it's untested in real CI so far. JSON output and exit
      codes are built in.
    </p></div>
  </details>
</div>

<style>
  .faq { margin-top: 5rem; }
  details {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
  details:first-of-type { border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border); }
  summary {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    padding: 1.05rem 0.25rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    color: var(--color-foreground);
  }
  summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  summary:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
  .chev {
    width: 1rem;
    height: 1rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    color: var(--color-muted-foreground);
    transition: transform 0.2s ease;
  }
  details[open] .chev { transform: rotate(90deg); }
  details[open] summary { color: var(--color-primary); }
  .a { padding: 0 0.25rem 1.15rem; }
  .a p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.98rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-muted-foreground);
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
</style>
